      "latin": "ērudītiō māchinārum, ērudītiōnis māchinārum f.",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "ours",
      "gloss": "the training of machines",
      "evidence": [
        "Cic. Q. Fr. 3.1.4: <span lang=\"la\">dē ejus ērudītiōne</span>, of a boy’s instruction (Lewis &amp; Short s.v. <span lang=\"la\">ērudītiō</span>)"
      ],
      "comment": "A description of ours: <span lang=\"la\">ērudītiō</span>, an instructing (Cicero), with the genitive of those instructed, as in Cicero’s <span lang=\"la\">dē ejus ērudītiōne</span>. It matches the verb used for training a model (see train). No reference work has a term for machine learning. Latin says the thing most naturally with a verb: <span lang=\"la\">māchina discit</span>, the machine learns."
    }
  ],
